#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166701 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29a290a2-bc9e-57bc-84f5-a21cba4848de" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Simpler Form Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "870077e3-99f9-52b9-ac39-26bbc3827466" :question/math-academy-id "e-9035" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\ln(x + y).\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z= x + y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form $z'(x) = f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x + y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 1 + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form $z'(x) = f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = \\ln z+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z) = \\ln z + 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166702 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35df900-489d-5f8e-9d9e-075352035790" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "d3145755-b414-57e5-8039-04ceb1a3a5f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9037" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x + y.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y) = x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 1 + \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form $z'(x) = f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = z+1.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{z+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{z+1}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|z+1| & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\ln{|x+y+1|}  = x + C\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y) = \\ln{|x+y+1|}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29a290a2-bc9e-57bc-84f5-a21cba4848de" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Simpler Form Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36446237-fb30-532e-b4bc-0f15cdd21661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482906} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb2a735-2e46-54e0-9874-7cbb3c9d2e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482976} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8c6b09-5053-55b0-9af4-ddde68c55268" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483005} {:answer/id #uuid "99ead3e1-62a9-5269-ad68-54edae96ed46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-{z}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483076} {:answer/id #uuid "deb53e97-ac5d-5842-80e7-e5bc8a58a041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "deb53e97-ac5d-5842-80e7-e5bc8a58a041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483187} :answer-field/id #uuid "093657e8-681a-5676-ae66-c18d57c5ed27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8deb854-1ac2-5d99-9d29-3afca56811fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-107786" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(2x-3y)^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=2x-3y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x-3y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-3\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-3{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=2-3{z}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29a290a2-bc9e-57bc-84f5-a21cba4848de" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Simpler Form Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "541bd680-2f74-58f2-acd0-641bbe38f1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{e}^{z+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241935} {:answer/id #uuid "5608d1ec-02c7-53d4-9bb6-a82a0192e759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{z}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241955} {:answer/id #uuid "89a733fb-19df-57d9-99b6-ccdc043ce3ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{z}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242391} {:answer/id #uuid "9a61b25a-ae9c-5664-8bf2-04704ce45070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{z+5}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242529} {:answer/id #uuid "cce85f44-267b-5248-a4f7-c8742520e8bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-5{e}^{z+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a61b25a-ae9c-5664-8bf2-04704ce45070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{z+5}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242529} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8b85e2a-be80-5ab9-ba75-e0dd0adbbf66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5d4c4f2-83ab-5c6a-b2e1-62527a7b057c" :question/math-academy-id "q-107797" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x+2y+5}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+2y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+2y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2} & ={e}^{z+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{z+5}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=2{e}^{z+5}+1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29a290a2-bc9e-57bc-84f5-a21cba4848de" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Simpler Form Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17bd8cc8-4bb4-5eff-ada9-0d3691acf532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482857} {:answer/id #uuid "46aef1dd-2eb5-515f-a6bb-f8f10224f799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482938} {:answer/id #uuid "8b31acd6-793b-5fd9-8007-45f8fd263cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483056} {:answer/id #uuid "d686bc1e-099d-5164-b83e-64876d5962cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(z-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483175} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb0492d-fea8-543e-b81d-269e86234098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(z+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17bd8cc8-4bb4-5eff-ada9-0d3691acf532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482857} :answer-field/id #uuid "662e1ec7-120e-5135-be30-35885522a76f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c5b13bf2-e455-5ecd-8fce-8c8ab9a50cfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-108799" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y).\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29a290a2-bc9e-57bc-84f5-a21cba4848de" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Simpler Form Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ce51685-f180-59a8-8398-4f2461a0c7e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-3{tan}^{2}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241867} {:answer/id #uuid "871089d9-f341-5092-8ccb-710312c1500d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-{tan}^{2}⁡(z-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242363} {:answer/id #uuid "87f2e6b6-aaad-513d-a404-4edede8cf981" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{tan}^{2}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242373} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf535e8-89f8-5e4c-8b07-ecd2d591cfa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-5{tan}^{2}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242676} {:answer/id #uuid "d418acdd-f22e-5d57-9607-c556cfe678d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+5\\operatorname{tan}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaf535e8-89f8-5e4c-8b07-ecd2d591cfa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-5{tan}^{2}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242676} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b0aca84-3c6c-5519-bd2a-14b78a11c9d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e81268a9-ddfd-5f92-b961-b0aa841e7817" :question/math-academy-id "q-108802" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={tan}^{2}⁡(3x-5y).\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=3x-5y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=3x-5y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3-5\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={tan}^{2}⁡z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3-5{tan}^{2}⁡z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=3-5{tan}^{2}⁡z.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35df900-489d-5f8e-9d9e-075352035790" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a44166c-ce03-507d-b55d-8543dd97c169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482862} {:answer/id #uuid "45350018-8a0e-55eb-b945-48b7b99e9399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+2y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482933} {:answer/id #uuid "602b69f4-c68b-5264-9f73-206c78d3fa7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+2y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482980} {:answer/id #uuid "e18dc9e8-49fb-5ea3-a407-51da0784fc23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483194} {:answer/id #uuid "fe722dbc-b929-55b0-84d4-b79092599856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe722dbc-b929-55b0-84d4-b79092599856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483238} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c6152f7-42f5-56fc-b75a-263cd69c9d19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "943c59c7-5044-5ab4-92f0-26722bf35b20" :question/math-academy-id "q-108838" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+2y)}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=C-2x,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+2y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z & =x+K \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡z & =C-2x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-2K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)=C-2x\\,\\,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35df900-489d-5f8e-9d9e-075352035790" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18c51cf0-b1a9-508f-89fe-cf5db654d586" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x-2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241397} {:answer/id #uuid "6cd7770d-b314-5404-b240-b5cfbc07fc7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x+y}(1-x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242156} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0c61fc-9d78-5132-bd22-6bbf13fd939d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x-y}(x+y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242285} {:answer/id #uuid "9e4df47b-1754-5c8f-aba3-53a7f538334e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242566} {:answer/id #uuid "f985d26b-0954-56c4-8beb-06d60810a431" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x+y}(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e0c61fc-9d78-5132-bd22-6bbf13fd939d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x-y}(x+y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242285} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1be1d49-45f2-5b7f-a18f-ad5c635c720b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a1afbf5f-1cd8-5fe7-a308-11eeef08401b" :question/math-academy-id "q-108841" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{e}^{x+y}-x-y}{x+y}.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=C-x,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\frac{{e}^{z}-z}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{e}^{z}}{z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{z}{{e}^{z}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int z{e}^{-z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts on the left side,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=z & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-z} & \\,⟹\\,v=-{e}^{-z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int z{e}^{-z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -z{e}^{-z}+\\int {e}^{-z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -z{e}^{-z}-{e}^{-z} & =x+C \\\\ -{e}^{-z}(z+1) & =x+K \\\\ {e}^{-z}(z+1) & =C-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n{e}^{-x-y}(x+y+1)=C-x\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)={e}^{-x-y}(x+y+1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483348 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29a290a2-bc9e-57bc-84f5-a21cba4848de" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Simpler Form Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17c2fb32-9b66-536c-8718-eae44692c7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482858} {:answer/id #uuid "3a46b666-9634-59d0-8c48-828293d7b287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{z}^{3}}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482911} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe306ca-1607-530b-9c80-4dc6d4945a1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483037} {:answer/id #uuid "d56be03b-3ecc-54bb-90cd-67c9da112068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483172} {:answer/id #uuid "dbdbed3c-05bf-59fb-bce3-b59de8af14d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{{z}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d56be03b-3ecc-54bb-90cd-67c9da112068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483172} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2de56b4-f1bc-5880-8c98-289d5a2bf62d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "10277519-c835-51d7-9d38-5cc5b84c2733" :question/math-academy-id "q-149712" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3-\\frac{1}{(3x-y)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=3x-y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=3x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =3-\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35df900-489d-5f8e-9d9e-075352035790" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c6a07b-8faf-5324-b53c-a2c58ed30c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{3x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272464} {:answer/id #uuid "1d7cc5e8-497a-5d6f-aec1-4ad07e072b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{y-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272649} {:answer/id #uuid "28394a0c-f284-5ba2-9531-bf15a477e453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272742} {:answer/id #uuid "a53691e1-9247-57ee-a1ef-92baf61ae110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{y-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273815} {:answer/id #uuid "ede43097-d715-52fa-8f73-02118dc5234a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{3x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28394a0c-f284-5ba2-9531-bf15a477e453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272742} :answer-field/id #uuid "d44bbdb7-74e5-579b-a1f0-5dcb97d86e01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2be2a98-4693-5e20-8a16-811d290d52b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-149721" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3-{e}^{y-3x}.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=y-3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3 & =3-{e}^{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-z}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ -\\int {e}^{-z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{-z} & =x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n{e}^{3x-y}=x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)={e}^{3x-y}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483347 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35df900-489d-5f8e-9d9e-075352035790" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c044647-5954-578d-89bb-bc6f400412e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x-y}+x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482829} {:answer/id #uuid "7df0459b-970a-5ced-a1bc-719c9769982d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x-y}}{x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483036} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0d519e-ab8f-5357-872e-b30413c6b3ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-y){e}^{x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483101} {:answer/id #uuid "be20304e-f6de-585f-9b63-d71900de2a74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{y-x}}{x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483131} {:answer/id #uuid "cb943e39-89bf-5763-b1be-0445659d52a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{y-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab0d519e-ab8f-5357-872e-b30413c6b3ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-y){e}^{x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483101} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d6cf9d9-7b5d-5a12-954e-3a9a145111eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0ebc5c12-2f6e-589d-af5a-42a020eb709e" :question/math-academy-id "q-149722" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{{e}^{y-x}}{x-y+1}.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-z}}{z+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{e}^{-z}}{z+1}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+1){e}^{z}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int (z+1){e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts on the left side,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=z+1 & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{z} & \\,⟹\\,v={e}^{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (z+1){e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ (z+1){e}^{z}-\\int {e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ (z+1){e}^{z}-{e}^{z} & =x+C \\\\ z{e}^{z} & =x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-y){e}^{x-y}=x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=(x-y){e}^{x-y}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483351 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35df900-489d-5f8e-9d9e-075352035790" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e716eff-4358-55b4-8561-ca1c8320c525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482891} {:answer/id #uuid "47901134-19b2-5653-9a75-9c013a497690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482939} {:answer/id #uuid "807b691f-3c19-516b-a689-d6024d5b6077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483038} {:answer/id #uuid "d60567b6-30a5-5299-bff7-543ccd03c29b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483174} {:answer/id #uuid "f6059557-3dac-50d0-8066-8dd8cc82e79c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d60567b6-30a5-5299-bff7-543ccd03c29b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483174} :answer-field/id #uuid "25217a67-7d84-5b06-8801-01edf4363fd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1808d5b9-dae6-5dea-b5bd-62c618289147" :question/math-academy-id "q-149723" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+2}-1.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\frac{x+C}{x+y},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+2}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+2)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+2)\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts on the left side,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+2 & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{z} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1 & \\,⟹\\,v=z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+2)\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ z(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+2)-\\int \\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ z(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+2)-z & =x+C \\\\ z(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+1) & =x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1) & =x+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1 & =\\frac{x+C}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1.$"}]}
